From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: domicile CI <ci@domicile.invalid>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 15:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: a chord pressed in a webview is told to its own
 page

Every monitor's page has a control channel, and ShortcutRegistry told a
claimed chord pressed in a <webview> to all of them -- so one Meta+Return
in a browser window opened a terminal per monitor, and a toggle toggled
once per monitor.

The binder now names the frame a channel is for, and the guest names the
frame it is embedded in; the registry tells only that page.
---
 chrome/browser/chrome_browser_interface_binders.cc | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/chrome/browser/chrome_browser_interface_binders.cc b/chrome/browser/chrome_browser_interface_binders.cc
index 3dd96f2e3f..8207c4b856 100644
--- a/chrome/browser/chrome_browser_interface_binders.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/chrome_browser_interface_binders.cc
@@ -550,7 +550,13 @@ void PopulateChromeFrameBinders(
                 // screen both are -- and read from the browser rather than
                 // asked of the page, which does not know which monitor it was
                 // put on and could only guess from its own geometry.
-                domicile::ScreenOf(frame)));
+                domicile::ScreenOf(frame),
+                // AND WHICH PAGE THIS IS, which is what a chord pressed in one
+                // of its <webview>s is told to: every monitor's page claims
+                // the same chords, and a press told to all of them is run
+                // once per monitor.
+                domicile::Page{frame->GetGlobalId().child_id.value(),
+                               frame->GetGlobalId().frame_routing_id}));
       }));
 
   // Domicile's engine fork: a <webview> asking for a guest to put behind it.
